WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.340
INTRO:
The Pirate Bay is likely one of the most infamous

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.249
websites in the world.

00:00:05.249 --> 00:00:10.219
Ever since it was launched in 2003, the Pirate
Bay has been one of the top places to pirate

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:12.929
movies, software, and games.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:17.730
As you would guess, this has enraged copyright
holders and prosecutors who have been trying

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.300
to shut down the site and punish pirates.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.800
But, despite how many times they take it down,
it seems like it’s always just a matter

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.980
of time until the site is back up and running.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.300
Considering this, you would think that the
key to solving the issue is addressing the

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.070
root problem by taking down the founders.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.520
But, prosecutors have tried this as well.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.790
They’ve chased the founders across the world,
thrown them in jail, and charged them with

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.120
every copyright infringement charge you can
think of.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.620
Yet, the Pirate Bay still lives on.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.110
In fact, one of the founders is actually happy
to do jail time in order to keep the Pirate

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:51.110
Bay alive.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:55.930
So, here’s how the Pirate Bay came to life,
what happened to the founders, and why prosecutors

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.160
have struggled to take it down.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.550
THE PIRATE BAY:
Taking a look back, the origins of the Pirate

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:08.770
Bay date back to a Swedish organization called
Piratbyran which means the Piracy Bureau.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:13.340
As the name suggests, the organization focused
on legalizing piracy through political connections,

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.350
petitioning, and lobbying.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.850
Most people at the organization felt that
information should be allowed to freely spread

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.510
across the internet and they questioned the
idea of intellectual property.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:28.219
In fact, some would even argue that piracy
is helpful to companies because it gets expensive

00:01:28.219 --> 00:01:32.409
software and games into the hands of people
who would have otherwise never tried it.

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:36.079
And once they get addicted to the said game
or software, they’re much more likely to

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:38.299
actually buy it the second or third time around.

00:01:38.299 --> 00:01:43.579
Now, I would never argue that piracy is moral,
but there’s no doubt that software like

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:48.139
Windows, Adobe’s Creative Cloud, and even
Grand Theft Auto wouldn’t be nearly as popular

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:50.679
as they are today without piracy.

00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:55.429
This line of reasoning was basically the framework
of Piratbyran, and in September of 2003, they

00:01:55.429 --> 00:01:57.770
would decide to take it to the next level.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:02.719
Three Piratebyran employees named Peter Sunde,
Gottfrid Svartholm, and Fredrik Neij launched

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:04.709
a file-sharing website called The Pirate Bay.

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:09.530
The idea was inspired by BitTorrent which
made its debut a few years before this.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:13.030
Originally, the Pirate Bay was run out of
servers in Mexico.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.550
Gottfrid convinced his employer who had servers
in Mexico to help them run the site.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.930
But, it didn’t take long for his employer
to back off.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.970
So, the founders were forced to bring the
site back home, and Gottfrid ran it using

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.320
his Pentium 3 laptop which only had 256 megabytes
of RAM.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.910
Despite the basic setup, given the limited
number of file-sharing sites back in 2003

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.760
though, it didn’t take long for pirates
to flood in.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:39.860
By the end of 2004, the Pirate Bay saw a total
of 1 million users and 60,000 files being

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:40.930
shared.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:45.490
As the site grew, the trio expanded their
operations by getting servers and databases,

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:49.530
and they transformed their laptop service
into an international hub for file sharing.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:53.780
And by 2006, everything under the sun was
being shared on the site whether that be music

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.370
and movies or software and games.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.260
And to make things worse, the Pirate Bay wasn’t
even trying to distance themselves from these

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.390
activities.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:05.960
Some other popular piracy sites like Mega.nz
try to put up a good guy persona in order

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.810
to minimize trouble with the law.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:10.190
But that wasn’t the case with the Pirate
Bay.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.920
These guys were proud to enable free information
sharing across the internet, and they had

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.750
no intention of hiding behind some fake persona.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:20.260
And this attitude became extremely apparent
to authorities after they sent out dozens

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.340
of copyright infringement and cease and desist
notices which resulted in no action from the

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.340
founders.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.750
The police tried to just ignore the new site
for many years, but as the site continued

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:33.010
to grow in popularity, they started to receive
more and more pressure to take it down.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:36.730
And in 2006, they finally decided to crackdown.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:41.540
BACKFIRE:
I’m sure the founders always expected trouble

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.090
with the authorities given the nature of what
they were doing.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.500
But, I’m not sure if they expected the force
at which prosecutors would hit back.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:53.430
On May 31, 2006, 65 police officers raided
the Pirate Bay’s data center and shut down

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.430
their servers.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.570
None of the founders were arrested, but it
was made pretty clear that the site should

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.410
not be restarted.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:03.440
As you would guess though, the founders ignored
these demands.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.560
They went ahead and got new servers in an
unspecified location in the Netherlands and

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:10.770
the Pirate Bay was back up and running within
just 3 days.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.970
Not only did the raid not take down the website,
but it actually led to more people using the

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:15.970
website.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:20.259
You see, the police raid became international
news, and even the New York Times ran a story

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:21.359
about it.

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:25.240
Aside from driving more users to the website,
these news articles fueled an international

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.440
movement amongst internet nerds.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.110
One hacker went ahead and hacked into Sweden’s
national police website, Polisen.se, and took

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:33.330
down the website.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:38.159
And, just as the police website was restored,
the government website was taken down.

00:04:38.159 --> 00:04:42.720
Clearly, these were extremely irresponsible
moves, but they definitely made a statement.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.370
Shortly after the raid, the Pirate Bay grew
to be the 465th most visited website in the

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.879
world, and some lawyers even jumped onto the
founders' side.

00:04:50.879 --> 00:04:54.550
The lawyers accused the police of unfairly
impounding every server in sight during the

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:55.550
raid.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:59.590
You see, the servers that were impounded weren’t
only responsible for running the Pirate Bay,

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.159
but also responsible for running dozens of
small websites and businesses.

00:05:03.159 --> 00:05:07.860
So, police had wronged all these businesses
in the name of taking down one website.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.460
The lawyers also argued that the Swedish police
didn’t have a justifiable reason to conduct

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.270
the raid in the first place.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:16.590
They accused the Swedish government of giving
in to American political pressure instead

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.389
of carrying out law and order.

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:22.150
The government of course strongly denied these
charges and these cases didn’t really go

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:23.150
anywhere.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:27.130
But, the government had a nightmare dealing
with all this unexpected negative PR.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:31.379
Initially, they expected that they would be
hailed as the heroes fighting against piracy,

00:05:31.379 --> 00:05:33.430
but instead, they were made out to be the
villains.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:38.819
The thing is, most people have pirated media
or software at some point during their lives.

00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:44.409
Statistically, 52% of online users have watched
pirated videos even though 59% of users are

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:48.150
aware that downloading and streaming pirated
videos is illegal.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.659
And that’s just the people who admitted
to it.

00:05:50.659 --> 00:05:54.539
As a result, when the news came out that The
Pirate Bay had been taken down, it was not

00:05:54.539 --> 00:05:57.449
like the average person was jumping up and
down in excitement.

00:05:57.449 --> 00:06:02.370
The more realistic reaction was probably,
oh well, it was nice while it lasted.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.879
Considering this, only a very small portion
of people went out of their way to support

00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:07.430
the Swedish government’s actions.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:11.449
And, without large public support, the government
couldn’t just raid the Pirate Bay again

00:06:11.449 --> 00:06:14.460
cause that would just increase its popularity
even more.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:19.729
So, they decided to address the root by taking
down the founders.

00:06:19.729 --> 00:06:24.470
ON THE RUN:
On April 17, 2009, the three founders Peter,

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.439
Fredrik, and Gottfrid as well as the server
provider Carl Lundstrom were found guilty

00:06:28.439 --> 00:06:32.639
of assistance to copyright infringement and
sentenced to 1 year in prison each.

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:37.779
They were also ordered to pay a fine worth
30 million Swedish kronor or $4.3 million.

00:06:37.779 --> 00:06:41.479
The squad appealed the verdict arguing that
Sweden gave in to political pressure.

00:06:41.479 --> 00:06:45.449
This actually decreased each of their sentences
by a few months, but it increased the fine

00:06:45.449 --> 00:06:47.580
to 46 million kronor, or $6.6 million.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:52.039
This didn’t really matter to the founders
though, as they had no intention of paying

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:53.039
the fine.

00:06:53.039 --> 00:06:56.270
Peter actually held up a sign during the press
conference that followed the verdict which

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.719
said I owe you 31 million kroner.

00:06:58.719 --> 00:07:02.050
He followed up this statement by suggesting
that this is all the government was going

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.050
to get.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:07.669
He claimed that he didn’t have any money
and even if he did, he’d rather burn everything

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:11.040
and not even give them the ashes.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.680
Carl and Peter didn’t really resist the
arrest much further and they simply gave in.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.050
But, the same could not be said about Gottfrid
and Fredrik who went on the run.

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:22.999
Gottfrid ran away to Cambodia which had a
no extradition policy to Sweden.

00:07:22.999 --> 00:07:28.300
But, despite the policy, the Cambodian police
arrested Gottfrid on August 30, 2012, and

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.629
deported him back to Sweden.

00:07:29.629 --> 00:07:34.469
There’s been some speculation that Sweden
and Cambodia had an inside deal to extradite

00:07:34.469 --> 00:07:35.469
Gottfrid.

00:07:35.469 --> 00:07:39.160
Just 6 days after Gottfrid was arrested, the
Swedish government announced a 400 million

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.909
kroner grant for Cambodia.

00:07:40.909 --> 00:07:45.259
So, it is awfully suspicious, but all we can
do is speculate.

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:49.550
Once Gottfrid was back in Sweden, he served
his jail sentence at the Mariefred prison,

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:51.839
but the police didn’t just stop right there.

00:07:51.839 --> 00:07:56.199
They also piled on hacking and fraud charges
which led to a total 3-year sentence.

00:07:56.199 --> 00:08:00.409
But eventually, in September of 2015, Gottfrid
was released.

00:08:00.409 --> 00:08:04.960
And finally, as for Fredrik, he was able to
evade the police for even longer than Gottfrid.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.059
He had fled to Laos/Thailand and he had built
a life in both of these countries.

00:08:09.059 --> 00:08:13.599
Honestly, I don’t know why he didn’t just
choose one or the other as this dual life

00:08:13.599 --> 00:08:16.610
resulted in him crossing the border on a regular
basis.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:21.469
And during one of these border crossings in
November of 2014, he was arrested and deported.

00:08:21.469 --> 00:08:25.189
Fortunately for Fredrik, his prison sentence
was far less severe than Gottfrid’s coming

00:08:25.189 --> 00:08:26.560
in at 10 months.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.159
Fredrik actually claims that prison wasn’t
even that bad and that it was well worth it

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:31.529
for the Pirate Bay.

00:08:31.529 --> 00:08:35.260
Apparently, he was the only one in the prison
that was there for a virtual crime.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.459
So, the guards weren’t as tough on him.

00:08:37.459 --> 00:08:41.039
He says that he was able to smuggle in USB
sticks with movies on them and watch them

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:42.630
on his prison TV.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:46.990
He did miss his friends and family, but he
received dozens of letters from fans of the

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:50.269
Pirate Bay which he says helped him get through
the 10 months.

00:08:50.269 --> 00:08:54.120
Now, that all of the founders were behind
bars, the police could finally shut down the

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.680
Pirate Bay once and for all, or so they thought.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:03.720
UNSTOPPABLE LEGACY:
On December 9, 2014, the Swedish police raided

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:08.410
the Pirate Bay once again and impounded all
of their servers, computers, and equipment.

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:12.259
With the founders out of the game, this must
be the end right?

00:09:12.259 --> 00:09:17.170
Well, just 4 days after the Pirate Bay was
taken down, a torrent site called Isohunt

00:09:17.170 --> 00:09:21.570
launched a website called oldpiratebay.org
which mirrored all the content on The Pirate

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:22.570
Bay.

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:25.610
And this is when the prosecutors realized
that they had lost the game forever.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:30.060
Here’s the thing, the Pirate Bay doesn’t
actually host any files themselves.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.639
They just connect peers from around the world
using links.

00:09:32.639 --> 00:09:35.449
So, all the Pirate Bay is storing is links.

00:09:35.449 --> 00:09:39.680
The contents of the entire website can be
stored within a gigabyte if not less, and

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.940
there are thousands of people making copies
of the website on a daily basis.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:47.291
So, even if authorities take down one copy
of the site, it’s not very difficult to

00:09:47.291 --> 00:09:50.870
upload the gigabyte of data to a new server
and get a new domain.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.170
And that’s why it’s impossible to truly
take down the Pirate Bay.

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:57.800
Considering this, prosecutors have basically
given up on taking down the Pirate Bay as

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.620
there’s simply no single person to hold
accountable.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.000
In the meantime, copyright holders have shifted
their efforts to working with internet service

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:05.940
providers to cut internet to pirates.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.709
But, this has just led to pirates using VPNs.

00:10:08.709 --> 00:10:13.430
Anyway, as for the founders today, Peter went
ahead and founded a Patreon-type service called

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:14.480
Flattr.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.850
Instead of donating to creators though, the
service is used to donate to websites and

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:21.410
projects that better society and reveal corruption
like WikiLeaks.

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:25.280
Aside from Flattr, Peter has also given several
speeches and interviews regarding his various

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:26.790
views on the world.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:30.720
Gottfrid and Fredrik on the other hand don’t
have nearly as big of a public image.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.040
In fact, they have no public presence, and
they simply disappeared into the depths of

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.120
the internet.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:39.500
After Fredrik was released, he said that he
was just gonna get an IT job and settle in

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:40.500
Laos.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.800
So, that’s presumably what he’s doing
now.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.110
And that’s what happened to the Pirate Bay’s
founders and why the Pirate Bay can never

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:47.540
be taken down.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.320
Do you guys think piracy is moral?

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:51.079
Comment that down below.

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:54.800
Also, drop a like if you’ve ever downloaded
something that you shouldn’t have.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:58.660
